WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.900
SSH keys are often used by CI/CD jobs, Git operations, deployment scripts, artifact synchronization jobs, monitoring checks, and remote administration tasks.

2
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:16.200
These environments create storage and exposure risks that differ from an interactive shell.

3
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:27.580
A key may be injected into a runner, copied into a workspace, cached in a container layer, printed in debug output, or reused across environments because it was easy to configure once.

4
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:32.580
Git deploy keys should be scoped to the repository and workflow that need them.

5
00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:38.760
A key that only needs read access to one repository should not have broader platform access.

6
00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:43.500
Deployment and artifact-transfer keys should be scoped to their target environment and task.

7
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:51.600
Production keys should be separated from development and staging keys so a lower-environment job does not accidentally gain production reach.

8
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.400
Build-time and runtime key material should be handled separately.

9
00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:05.580
Keys used during image builds can be captured in layers, caches, package metadata, or build logs if the process is not designed carefully.

10
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.640
Runtime keys should be injected at runtime through controlled mechanisms where practical.

11
00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:16.580
Temporary key files should avoid artifact paths, shared caches, and broad workspaces.

12
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:22.700
Secret masking helps reduce accidental log exposure, but it is not a complete boundary.

13
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:30.640
Derived values, file names, debug output, copied workspaces, or untrusted jobs can still create risk.

14
00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:38.580
Automation should clean up temporary key material, avoid overbroad environment access, and log enough to support review without exposing secrets.

15
00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:44.040
CI/CD SSH keys should be treated as production credentials when they can affect production systems.
